3. STEP-BY-STEP OPERATIONAL PROCEDURES
PROCEDURE A: Daily Central Batch Requisition, Precision Scaling, Issuance & Line Execution
* Step 1: Production Requisition Submission (Form-PRO-001)
   1. The Station Baker or Outlet In-Charge submits Form-PRO-001 via the dedicated terminal before the scheduled shift cutoff.
   2. The requisition must specify:
      * Product Line SKU and commercial item title.
      * Target batch multiplier and required finished unit count.
      * Authorized Administration Approval Code / Costing Reference.
   3. Requisitions for unlisted, uncosted, or experimental varieties are automatically rejected by the system.
* Step 2: Recipe Matching & Sizing by Romeo B. Malusay
   1. Cross-reference Form-PRO-001 against the Official Master Formulation Register cards corresponding to the target production facility or retail outlet (Laurice Delicacies vs. Pan de Verde specifications).
   2. Compute exact component gram weights based on approved baker's percentages (flour basis = 100.0%).
   3. Generate the internal Raw Material Pull List and populate target weights onto Form-ING-001 (Ingredient Batching, Scaling & Issuance Record).
* Step 3: Upstream Warehouse Material Transfer (Jade Q. Dela Cruz)
   1. Romeo B. Malusay presents Form-ING-001 at the warehouse staging window / Dutch door.
   2. Jade Q. Dela Cruz verifies stock and issues raw materials strictly under First-Expired, First-Out (FEFO) inventory rotation rules (SOP-WAR-001):
      * Dry Room 1: Dry commodities (flours, sugars, starches) must originate from clean, pallet-elevated, sealed High-Density Polyethylene (HDPE) bins equipped with dedicated scoops.
      * Cold Room (0°C to -4°C): Dairy and temperature-sensitive specialty fats.
      * Wet Room / Tempering Staging: Butter and shortening blocks must complete verified tempering to 18°C–20°C prior to issuance.
   3. Jade logs raw material supplier lot numbers, bag/container quantities, and release timestamps onto Form-ING-001 before transferring materials across the hatch.
* Step 4: Mechanical Sifting, Scale Calibration & Precision Scaling
   1. Calibration Verification: Romeo inspects digital bench scales against government calibration seals (Seal Nos. #798073 and #798074), performs zero-point verification, and logs data on Form-CAL-001.
   2. Mechanical Sifting: Pass all dry flour through a 40-mesh stainless steel sifting screen to aerate the flour, eliminate compact lumps, and intercept foreign matter.
   3. Unitized Batch Kitting: Scale ingredients into sanitized, color-coded containers and attach a completed Batch Identification Tag detailing:
      * Product SKU Code and Commercial Item Name
      * Batch Number, Lot Traceability Codes, and Preparation Date
      * Net Component Gram Weights (Flour, Sugar, Fats, Additives, Leaveners)
* Step 5: Grey Zone Transfer, Mixing & Thermal Execution
   1. Transfer sealed batch kits across the Medium Care (Grey) Zone airlock to designated line mixers.
   2. The receiving baker inspects kit seals, checks tag quantities against production requirements, and countersigns the issuance log on Form-ING-001.
   3. Mixing Dynamics: Execute standardized spiral mixing cycles, monitoring shear friction factors to achieve a finished dough exit temperature strictly between 26°C and 28°C.
   4. Thermal Lethality (CCP-1): Bake loaves to reach an internal core product temperature exceeding 95°C, ensuring full microbial lethality without scorching or structural collapse.
   5. Yield Reconciliation: At shift conclusion, outlet bakers record finished unit output on the Digital Shift Yield Log, reconciling actual output against theoretical formulation yields. Discrepancies exceeding $\pm 2.0\%$ trigger an immediate supervisory audit.
PROCEDURE B: New Product Development (NPD) & Formulation Modification Protocol
* Stage 1: Proposal Submission (Form-NPD-001)
   1. Bakers seeking to modify existing formulas (hydration shift, sweetness adjustment, fat substitution) or propose original recipes submit Form-NPD-001.
   2. Proposals must document item category, target outlet, full formula in baker's percentages, target shelf-life, and commercial concept justification. Floor testing without this submission is prohibited.
* Stage 2: Technical, Rheological & Engineering Review (Joint Evaluation)
   1. Master Baker Rheological Validation (Alwen Hermogeno – Master Head Baker):
      * Hydration & Extensibility: Evaluates water absorption rates and gluten matrix structure using the standardized windowpane membrane test.
      * Mixing Cycle Feasibility: Establishes spiral mixer rotational shear duration, optimal speed steps, and dough friction factors to guarantee mass exit temperatures between 26°C and 28°C.
      * Proofing Behavior: Analyzes yeast gas production rates and dough expansion volume curves to establish maximum proofing dwell times and prevent structural collapse.
   2. Engineering & Thermal Profiling (Ramil Capada – Building & Equipment Manager):
      * Senior Craft & Mechanical Oversight: Leverages historical Master Head Baker domain knowledge combined with physical plant engineering to audit mixer gear stress, motor capacity, and line feasibility.
      * Thermal Profile Matching: Determines oven compatibility based on crust and crumb requirements:
         * Deck Oven Radiant Profile: Analyzes bottom conduction and radiant top heat for artisan loaves and hearth crusts requiring stable base heat transfer.
         * Rotary Rack Convection Airflow: Evaluates laminar heated airflow for moisture evacuation efficiency, uniform expansion, and balanced crust caramelization.
      * Lethality Validation (CCP-1): Confirms thermal transfer parameters deliver core internal product temperatures > 95°C across all oven rack positions.
   3. Food Safety & Stability Evaluation (Grace P. Suico – FSCO & QA Control Director):
      * Audits formulation against allergen segregation standards and cGMP regulations.
      * Enforces water activity thresholds:  for dry, shelf-stable delicacies (Biscocho, Otap) and verifies mold-inhibition hurdles for moist crumb lines.
      * Formulates packaging barrier specifications and regulatory labeling copy.
   * Stage 3: Costing & Commercial Feasibility (Jeanifer S. Sauping & Maria Charito Basoc)
   1. Map component weights to live raw material purchasing costs from Jade Q. Dela Cruz's warehouse logs.
   2. Factor in operational scrap allowances (capped at < 2.0%), secondary packaging materials, and utility overhead.
   3. Establish batch yields, wholesale transfer costs, and recommended retail prices (SRP) to safeguard margin targets.
   * Stage 4: Administrative Authorization & System Integration (Angelina P. Sun)
   1. The Head of Administration evaluates the combined technical, engineering, safety, and financial dossier.
   2. Upon written executive approval:
   * Assign an Official System SKU Code (e.g., BRD-COMM-001).
   * Integrate master formulation cards into Romeo B. Malusay’s active register.
   * Unlock requisitioning permissions on Form-PRO-001.
PROCEDURE C: Controlled Test-Bake Protocol (Trial Batches)
   1. Trial runs for sensory evaluation require an approved Sample Batch Request countersigned by the Master Head Baker.
   2. Romeo B. Malusay scales a single micro-batch, marking containers with high-visibility "R&D / TRIAL BATCH ONLY" tape.
   3. R&D trial bakes must remain strictly isolated from commercial production lines.
   4. Commercial Prohibition: Under no circumstances may R&D trial bakes be distributed, placed on retail display shelves, or sold to customers. Unused trial bakes must be logged and discarded under QA oversight.
